---
import { studies } from "../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";
const slug = "sentiment-topology";
const study = studies.find(s => s.slug === slug);
const related = studies.filter(s => s.slug !== slug).slice(0, 3);
const dateStr = new Date(study.date).toLocaleDateString("en-US", { month: "long", year: "numeric" });
const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "ScholarlyArticle",
  "@id": `${Astro.site}/studies/${slug}`,
  "headline": study.title, "datePublished": new Date(study.date).toISOString(),
  "abstract": study.abstract, "keywords": study.keywords.join(", "),
  "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Justin Chua", "url": "https://juscxnn.github.io",
    "sameAs": ["https://linkedin.com/in/justincxa", "https://github.com/juscxnn"] }
});
---

<StudyLayout title={study.title} description={study.abstract} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <article class="study-article" data-astro-cid="s">
    <header class="study-header" data-astro-cid="s">
      <p class="study-date" data-astro-cid="s">{dateStr}</p>
      <h1 class="study-title" data-astro-cid="s">{study.title}</h1>
      <p class="study-abstract" data-astro-cid="s">{study.abstract}</p>
      <div class="study-keywords" data-astro-cid="s">
        {study.keywords.map(kw => <span class="study-kw" data-astro-cid="s">{kw}</span>)}
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="study-section" id="introduction" data-astro-cid="s">
      <h2 data-astro-cid="s">1. Introduction</h2>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">Multi-agent language model systems have proliferated rapidly over the past two years. Frameworks like AutoGen, ChatDev, MetaGPT, and LangChain's agent primitives all share a common interaction model: round-robin turn-taking. One agent speaks, then the next, then the next, in a fixed or rule-governed sequence. Each agent generates a message in isolation, conditioned on the conversation history up to that point but with no knowledge of what other agents are about to say. The result is a discourse that is legible, structured, and completely unlike any real human conversation.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">This is not a cosmetic complaint. Real online communities &#8212; the kind Nichesim aims to simulate for product teams, brand strategists, and community managers &#8212; do not operate on polite turn-taking. Real discourse is messy. People interrupt each other. Three people pile onto one bad take simultaneously. Two users form a spontaneous alliance and back each other up across multiple threads. Someone derails the entire conversation with a pet peeve. A callback to an inside joke from fifty messages ago lands perfectly and shifts the emotional register of the room. None of this can be generated by a system that asks agents to take turns one at a time while blind to what their peers are about to say.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">We introduce <strong data-astro-cid="s">burst generation</strong>, a scene-directed multi-agent dialogue architecture that addresses this fundamental limitation. Instead of generating one message at a time, burst generation produces multi-message exchanges &#8212; groups of 2 to 4 messages from different agents &#8212; in a single LLM call. Each burst is orchestrated by a <em data-astro-cid="s">scene directive</em> that specifies the social dynamic at play, the agents involved, and the emotional arc the exchange should follow. The result is conversation that exhibits genuine emergent social dynamics: pile-ons, alliances, debates, derailments, and callbacks that would be impossible to produce under a round-robin regime.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-section" id="algorithm" data-astro-cid="s">
      <h2 data-astro-cid="s">2. The Burst Generation Algorithm</h2>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">The burst generation algorithm operates in three phases per cycle: participant selection, dynamic assignment, and scene execution. In the participant selection phase, the system chooses 2 to 3 agents from the active persona pool to participate in the forthcoming burst. Selection is not random. Each agent's selection weight is computed as a weighted sum of four factors designed to surface the most contextually relevant and conversationally interesting voices.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">The first factor is <strong data-astro-cid="s">opinion strength</strong>, derived from the agent's belief vector. Agents with extreme beliefs on dimensions relevant to the trigger topic are more likely to be selected, because strong opinions drive engagement. The second is <strong data-astro-cid="s">trigger topic presence</strong>: if the conversation has recently touched on a topic that maps to one of the agent's defined trigger topics, that agent receives a substantial boost. The third is <strong data-astro-cid="s">expert relevance</strong>, which activates during the deep-dive phase of a conversation and gives weight to agents whose expertise aligns with the current thread. The fourth is a <strong data-astro-cid="s">balance penalty</strong> that suppresses agents who have already dominated recent turns, preventing a single voice from monopolising the conversation. A small random jitter is added to keep selection from becoming deterministic and repetitive.</p>
      <pre data-astro-cid="s"><code data-astro-cid="s">weighted_score = 
  base_weight(0.3)
  + opinion_strength(abs(belief-50)/50 * 0.3)
  + trigger_topic_match(0.5 if present)
  + expert_boost(0.4 if deep_dive phase)
  + balance_penalty(-0.4 if over-represented)
  + random_jitter(0-0.5)</code></pre>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">Once the participating agents are selected, the system rolls a social dynamic from a weighted distribution. The available dynamics are: <strong data-astro-cid="s">PILE-ON</strong> (multiple people gang up on one position, some harsh and some apologetic), <strong data-astro-cid="s">ALLIANCE</strong> (two personas back each other up, reinforcing a shared perspective), <strong data-astro-cid="s">DEBATE</strong> (direct disagreement with substantive counter-arguments), <strong data-astro-cid="s">DERAIL</strong> (someone introduces a tangent or pet peeve that shifts the conversation), <strong data-astro-cid="s">CALLBACK</strong> (a reference to an earlier moment or inside joke that recontextualises the current thread), and <strong data-astro-cid="s">NORMAL</strong> (organic reactions without a strong organising dynamic). The distribution of these dynamics shifts by conversation phase, as described in Section 3.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">With participants and dynamic selected, the system constructs a <em data-astro-cid="s">scene directive</em> &#8212; a natural language instruction that frames the entire multi-message exchange as a directed scene. For example: "SCENE: A pile-on. Most people gang up against one take. @user1, @user2 come at the same point from different angles &#8212; some harsh, some 'sorry but...'. @user3 doubles down." This directive is prepended to the conversation context, and the LLM is instructed to generate 2 to 4 messages from the selected agents in sequence. Crucially, the LLM sees the entire scene at once and can coordinate the messages accordingly &#8212; escalating tension across the burst, maintaining each agent's linguistic style, and using direct @-mentions to reflect the relational structure of the dynamic. The generated messages are saved sequentially with jittered timestamps (&#177;5-60 seconds) to simulate natural typing cadence.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-section" id="scene-directives" data-astro-cid="s">
      <h2 data-astro-cid="s">3. Scene Directives as Social Primitives</h2>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">Scene directives are the key abstraction that distinguishes burst generation from prior multi-agent architectures. Rather than encoding interaction logic in code &#8212; with explicit rules for when an agent should reply, how it should format its response, or what knowledge it should access &#8212; burst generation encodes interaction logic in natural language framing that the LLM interprets holistically. A scene directive is not a prompt template. It is a narrative instruction that tells the model <em data-astro-cid="s">what kind of social moment</em> is unfolding and lets the model fill in the specific linguistic realisation.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">The distribution of scene dynamics is not static. It shifts across three conversation phases to mirror the natural arc of real discussions. During the <strong data-astro-cid="s">engagement phase</strong> (the first third of a simulation), the system uses an aggressive distribution: 25% pile-on, 25% debate, 20% alliance, 20% normal, 5% derail, and 5% callback. This produces the high-energy, position-staking discourse characteristic of a community encountering a new topic. During the <strong data-astro-cid="s">deep-dive phase</strong> (the middle third), the distribution shifts toward substantive exchange: 35% debate, 25% alliance, 25% normal, 10% pile-on, and 5% derail. During the <strong data-astro-cid="s">wind-down phase</strong> (the final third), the distribution relaxes to 55% normal, 15% callback, 15% derail, 10% alliance, and 5% debate, with pile-ons largely absent. This phase-dependent scheduling gives the simulated conversation a recognisable narrative structure &#8212; it heats up, digs in, and cools down &#8212; without requiring any explicit state machine or hand-authored transition logic.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-section" id="efficiency" data-astro-cid="s">
      <h2 data-astro-cid="s">4. Computational Efficiency</h2>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">The practical motivation for burst generation is latency and cost. Round-robin multi-agent systems require exactly one LLM call per message. In a typical Nichesim simulation producing 80 messages across 8 to 12 personas, a round-robin approach would require 80 or more API calls &#8212; each incurring network round-trip time, token processing overhead, and per-call costs. With burst generation producing an average of 2.8 messages per call, the same 80-message simulation requires approximately 25 to 30 calls, a reduction of 60&#8211;75%.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">This efficiency gain is what makes real-time streaming feasible. Nichesim's user-facing interface displays messages as they are generated, with each burst appearing as a rapid cluster of activity followed by a pause. The cadence produced by 2-to-4-message bursts with jittered timestamps is perceptually indistinguishable from real-time human conversation at the skim-reading speeds typical of social media consumption. Round-robin systems, by contrast, produce a plodding one-at-a-time rhythm that users consistently rate as robotic and unengaging in our internal testing.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="study-section" id="prior-art" data-astro-cid="s">
      <h2 data-astro-cid="s">5. Comparison with Prior Art</h2>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">AutoGen (Microsoft Research, 2023) popularised the multi-agent conversation pattern in production LLM systems, with agents participating in group chats mediated by a central manager. ChatDev (Qian et al., 2023) applied a similar round-robin pattern to software development workflows, with agents taking turns as analysts, programmers, and testers in a waterfall-inspired sequence. MetaGPT (Hong et al., 2023) extended this with structured intermediate outputs (requirements documents, design specs) passed between agent turns. LangChain's agent abstractions generalise the pattern further, treating agents as tools that can be called in sequence by a reasoning loop. All of these systems share the same architectural assumption: one agent, one turn, one message.</p>
      <p data-astro-cid="s">Burst generation is, to our knowledge, the first multi-agent dialogue architecture to abandon sequential turn-taking in favour of social scene framing. It treats the LLM not as an agent in a conversation but as a director staging a scene with multiple actors simultaneously. This shifts the unit of generation from the individual message to the social moment, and the organising principle from logical turn order to narrative dynamics. The result is synthetic conversation that exhibits the messiness, spontaneity, and emergent structure of real human discourse &#8212; precisely the properties that round-robin systems, by design, eliminate.</p>
    </section>

    <aside class="study-backlink" data-astro-cid="s">
      <p class="study-backlink-label" data-astro-cid="s">This work powers {study.backlinkLabel} in production.</p>
      <a class="study-backlink-link" href={study.backlink} rel="noopener" data-astro-cid="s">Try it at {study.backlinkLabel} &rarr;</a>
    </aside>
  </article>

  <section id="related" data-astro-cid="s">
    <h2 data-astro-cid="s">Related Studies</h2>
    <div class="study-related-grid" data-astro-cid="s">
      {related.map(r => {
        const rd = new Date(r.date).toLocaleDateString("en-US", { month: "long", year: "numeric" });
        return (
          <a class="study-related-card" href={`/studies/${r.slug}`} data-astro-cid="s">
            <p class="study-related-date" data-astro-cid="s">{rd}</p>
            <h3 class="study-related-title" data-astro-cid="s">{r.title}</h3>
            <p class="study-related-abstract" data-astro-cid="s">{r.abstract}</p>
            <span class="study-related-link" data-astro-cid="s">Read &rarr;</span>
          </a>
        );
      })}
    </div>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>

<style>
  .study-article[data-astro-cid="s"] { max-width: 680px; margin: 0 auto; padding-block: 32px 48px; }
  .study-header[data-astro-cid="s"] { margin-bottom: 40px; }
  .study-date[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.1em; color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 12px; }
  .study-title[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.2rem); font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.15; letter-spacing: -0.02em; color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 16px; }
  .study-abstract[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--muted); max-width: 58ch; margin: 0 0 14px; }
  .study-keywords[data-astro-cid="s"] { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px; }
  .study-kw[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10.5px; letter-spacing: 0.03em; color: var(--green); background: var(--chip-bg); border: 1px solid var(--hairline-soft); border-radius: 5px; padding: 2px 8px; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] { margin-top: 36px; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] h2[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 1.2rem; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -0.01em; color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 14px; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] p[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 16px; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] p[data-astro-cid="s"] strong[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-weight: 500; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] em[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-style: italic; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] pre[data-astro-cid="s"] { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 8px; padding: 18px 20px; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 12.5px; line-height: 1.6; margin: 18px 0 22px; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] code[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.88em; background: var(--chip-bg); border-radius: 4px; padding: 1px 5px; }
  .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] pre[data-astro-cid="s"] code[data-astro-cid="s"] { background: none; padding: 0; border-radius: 0; font-size: 12.5px; color: var(--ink); }
  .study-backlink[data-astro-cid="s"] { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: baseline; justify-content: space-between; gap: 10px; background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 20px 22px; margin-top: 40px; }
  .study-backlink-label[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 14px; color: var(--ink); margin: 0; }
  .study-backlink-link[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 13px; color: var(--green); white-space: nowrap; }
  .study-backlink-link[data-astro-cid="s"]:hover { text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 4px; }
  #related[data-astro-cid="s"] { margin-top: 44px; }
  .study-related-grid[data-astro-cid="s"] { display: grid; gap: 10px; grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  .study-related-card[data-astro-cid="s"] { display: flex; flex-direction: column; background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 18px; transition: border-color 0.15s, transform 0.15s; }
  .study-related-card[data-astro-cid="s"]:hover { border-color: var(--green); transform: translateY(-1px); }
  .study-related-date[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10.5px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .study-related-title[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 1.02rem; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.3; color: var(--ink); margin: 0 0 8px; }
  .study-related-abstract[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 13.5px; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 12px; }
  .study-related-link[data-astro-cid="s"] { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11.5px; color: var(--green); margin-top: auto; }
  @media (max-width: 639px) {
    .study-article[data-astro-cid="s"] { padding-block: 24px 36px; padding-inline: 8px; }
    .study-section[data-astro-cid="s"] pre[data-astro-cid="s"] { padding: 14px 16px; font-size: 11.5px; }
    .study-backlink[data-astro-cid="s"] { flex-direction: column; }
  }
</style>
